1512		axis-parallel and diagonal components and, within each component, uniform
1513		weight on the index $i$, so each restricted distribution carries mixture
1514		weight $\tfrac{1}{2m}$ in $D_{\mathrm{Line}}$. A nonnegative average that
1515		is at most $\delta$ therefore bounds each mixture component by
1516		$2m\delta$, and products of two such mixtures give the factor
1517		$(2m)^2 = 4m^2$. The instance \eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} is item
1518		2 applied to the conclusion of Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, whose
1519		$\simeq_{\delta_P}$ relation over two independent copies of the
1520		line-point distribution is precisely the statement that the left-hand
1521		side of \eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}, averaged over
1522		$D_{\mathrm{Line}} \times D_{\mathrm{Line}}$, is at most $\delta_P$.
